### What is the problem?

Minimum Window Substring finds the shortest substring of a text that contains all characters of a pattern including their required multiplicities. For source "ADOBECODEBANC" and pattern "ABC", the answer is "BANC" (length 4). This is one of the hardest sliding window problems and a classic interview question.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class MinWindowMatched {
    public static String minWindow(String s, String t) {
        if (s == null || t == null || s.length() < t.length()) return "";
        Map<Character, Integer> need = new HashMap<>();
        for (char c : t.toCharArray()) need.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);

        Map<Character, Integer> window = new HashMap<>();
        int required = need.size(), formed = 0;
        int left = 0, bestStart = 0, bestLen = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char rc = s.charAt(right);
            window.merge(rc, 1, Integer::sum);
            if (need.containsKey(rc) && window.get(rc).intValue() == need.get(rc).intValue()) {
                formed++;
            }
            while (formed == required) {
                if (right - left + 1 < bestLen) {
                    bestLen = right - left + 1;
                    bestStart = left;
                }
                char lc = s.charAt(left);
                window.merge(lc, -1, Integer::sum);
                if (need.containsKey(lc) && window.get(lc) < need.get(lc)) {
                    formed--;
                }
                left++;
            }
        }
        return bestLen == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? "" : s.substring(bestStart, bestStart + bestLen);
    }
}
```

### Why does this matter in banking?

The incident response team at the National Bank of India has a long event-code log and a set of required codes with minimum counts that together signal a specific failure. The team wants the shortest contiguous log slice containing them all so they can zoom straight to the root cause without reading the entire log.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dsa.MinWindowMatched;

public class LogAnalysisService {
    public void findIncident(String log, String required) {
        String window = MinWindowMatched.minWindow(log, required);
        System.out.println("Shortest incident window: '" + window + "'");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new LogAnalysisService().findIncident("ADOBECODEBANC", "ABC");
    }
}
```

```text
Shortest incident window: 'BANC'
```

### How does the formed counter provide O(1) validity?

Instead of rescanning the window each step to check whether all requirements are met, the formed counter answers in O(1). It only changes when a character crosses its required threshold on the way in or out. When adding a character whose window count just reached its need count, increment formed. When removing a character whose window count just dropped below its need count, decrement formed.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FormedCounterDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Pattern "AAB" needs: {A:2, B:1}
        Map<Character, Integer> need = new HashMap<>();
        need.put('A', 2); need.put('B', 1);
        int required = need.size(); // 2 distinct chars

        Map<Character, Integer> win = new HashMap<>();
        int formed = 0;

        // Add first A
        win.merge('A', 1, Integer::sum); // A:1, need A:2 ── not yet
        System.out.println("After A: formed=" + formed);

        // Add second A
        win.merge('A', 1, Integer::sum); // A:2, need A:2 ── formed++
        if (win.get('A').intValue() == need.get('A').intValue()) formed++;
        System.out.println("After AA: formed=" + formed);

        // Add B
        win.merge('B', 1, Integer::sum); // B:1, need B:1 ── formed++
        if (win.get('B').intValue() == need.get('B').intValue()) formed++;
        System.out.println("After AAB: formed=" + formed + " (required=" + required + ")");
    }
}
```

```text
After A: formed=0
After AA: formed=1
After AAB: formed=2 (required=2)
```

### How does contraction work?

Once formed equals required, the window is valid. Record it if it is the smallest so far. Then remove the character at the left edge: decrement its window count. If it was a required character and its count just dropped below the needed amount, decrement formed. Advance left and repeat while formed still equals required.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class ContractionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Simulating contraction on "ADOBECODEBANC" with pattern "ABC"
        // Once formed==3, try shrinking:
        // [0,5] "ADOBEC" len=6, remove A ── formed drops to 2, stop
        // Later: [9,12] "BANC" len=4, remove B ── formed drops to 2, stop
        // Best window: "BANC" (length 4)
        System.out.println(MinWindowMatched.minWindow("ADOBECODEBANC", "ABC"));
    }
}
```

```text
BANC
```

### What are the edge cases?

If the pattern is longer than the source, no valid window exists. If the pattern contains characters not in the source, no valid window exists. If the pattern has duplicates, the need map holds the required count and formed only increments at the exact threshold.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("'" + MinWindowMatched.minWindow("A", "AA") + "'");   // "" (need 2 A's)
        System.out.println("'" + MinWindowMatched.minWindow("ABC", "D") + "'");  // "" (D not in source)
        System.out.println("'" + MinWindowMatched.minWindow("A", "A") + "'");    // "A"
        System.out.println("'" + MinWindowMatched.minWindow("AA", "AA") + "'");  // "AA"
    }
}
```

```text
''
''
'A'
'AA'
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using a set instead of a count map fails when the pattern has duplicate characters. Comparing Integer objects with `==` instead of `.intValue()` fails for counts above 127. Forgetting to decrement formed only when the count drops strictly below the need leads to premature exit from the contraction loop.

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(|s| + |t|). Building the need map takes O(|t|). The sliding window processes each character at most twice: once when right adds it, once when left removes it. Space is O(|s| + |t|) for the two frequency maps.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you return all minimum windows of the same length?
- How does this differ from the fixed-size anagram sliding window?
- Can you optimize space by using a single count array with positive and negative values?
- What if the pattern allows wildcards?
